  "text": "We are given a parabola:\n\n$$\ny^2 = 4x\n$$\n\nWe are to find the **locus of the centers** $(a, b)$ of all **circles** that intersect the parabola at **two distinct points**, such that the **line segment connecting these two points** is **perpendicular to the tangent line** to the parabola at the **midpoint** of the chord.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the geometric condition\n\nLet the two points of intersection of the circle and the parabola be $ P_1 = (x_1, y_1) $ and $ P_2 = (x_2, y_2) $.\n\nWe are told:\n\n- The **line segment** $ P_1P_2 $ is **perpendicular** to the **tangent line** to the parabola at the **midpoint** of $ P_1P_2 $.\n\nLet’s denote the **midpoint** of $ P_1P_2 $ as:\n\n$$\nM = \\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nThe **tangent line to the parabola** at a point $ (x, y) $ on the parabola $ y^2 = 4x $ has slope:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2}{y}\n$$\n\nSo, the **slope of the tangent line at point $ M $** is $ \\frac{2}{y_M} $, where $ y_M = \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} $.\n\nNow, the **line segment $ P_1P_2 $** has direction vector:\n\n$$\n(x_2 - x_1, y_2 - y_1)\n$$\n\nSo, the **slope of the line segment** is:\n\n$$\nm_{P_1P_2} = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}\n$$\n\nWe are told that this line segment is **perpendicular** to the tangent line at the midpoint. So, the **product of the slopes** is $ -1 $:\n\n$$\n\\left( \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} \\right) \\cdot \\left( \\frac{2}{y_M} \\right) = -1\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} \\cdot \\frac{2}{y_M} = -1\n$$\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n- $ y_1^2 = 4x_1 $\n- $ y_2^2 = 4x_2 $\n\nWe can write:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\frac{y_1^2}{4}, \\quad x_2 = \\frac{y_2^2}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, the difference in x-coordinates is:\n\n$$\nx_2 - x_1 = \\frac{y_2^2 - y_1^2}{4} = \\frac{(y_2 - y_1)(y_2 + y_1)}{4}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the line segment is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{\\frac{(y_2 - y_1)(y_2 + y_1)}{4}} = \\frac{4}{y_2 + y_1}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the segment is $ \\frac{4}{y_1 + y_2} $\n\nNow, the slope of the tangent line at the midpoint is $ \\frac{2}{y_M} = \\frac{2}{(y_1 + y_2)/2} = \\frac{4}{y_1 + y_2} $\n\nSo the **slope of the segment is equal to the slope of the tangent line at the midpoint**, which contradicts the perpendicularity condition.\n\nWait — this suggests that the line segment is **parallel** to the tangent line, not perpendicular. So we must **re-express** the condition.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Re-express the perpendicularity condition\n\nWe are told that the **line segment** $ P_1P_2 $ is **perpendicular** to the **tangent line** at the **midpoint** of the chord.\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n- The **direction vector** of the line segment is $ \\vec{v} = (x_2 - x_1, y_2 - y_1) $\n- The **direction vector of the tangent line** at the midpoint $ M $ is $ \\vec{t} = (1, \\frac{2}{y_M}) $, since the slope is $ \\frac{2}{y_M} $\n\nFor the two vectors to be **perpendicular**, their **dot product must be zero**:\n\n$$\n(x_2 - x_1)(1) + (y_2 - y_1)\\left(\\frac{2}{y_M}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nRecall that $ y_M = \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} $, and $ x_2 - x_1 = \\frac{y_2^2 - y_1^2}{4} = \\frac{(y_2 - y_1)(y_2 + y_1)}{4} $\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y_2 - y_1)(y_2 + y_1)}{4} + (y_2 - y_1)\\left(\\frac{2}{\\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2}}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nSimplify the second term:\n\n$$\n(y_2 - y_1)\\left(\\frac{2}{\\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2}}\\right) = (y_2 - y_1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{y_1 + y_2}\n$$\n\nSo the entire equation is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y_2 - y_1)(y_2 + y_1)}{4} + \\frac{4(y_2 - y_1)}{y_1 + y_2} = 0\n$$\n\nFactor out $ (y_2 - y_1) $:\n\n$$\n(y_2 - y_1)\\left( \\frac{y_2 + y_1}{4} + \\frac{4}{y_1 + y_2} \\right) = 0\n$$\n\nSince $ y_2 \\ne y_1 $ (the points are distinct), we can divide by $ y_2 - y_1 $, and get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y_2 + y_1}{4} + \\frac{4}{y_1 + y_2} = 0\n$$\n\nLet’s denote $ s = y_1 + y_2 $. Then the equation becomes:\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{4} + \\frac{4}{s} = 0\n$$\n\nMultiply both sides by $ 4s $:\n\n$$\ns^2 + 16 = 0 \\Rightarrow s^2 = -16\n$$\n\nThis is **impossible**, since $ s = y_1 + y_2 $ is a real number (as $ y_1, y_2 $ are real), so $ s^2 = -16 $ is **not possible**.\n\nBut this is a contradiction. So there must be a **mistake** in our logic.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Re-express the condition more carefully\n\nLet’s go back to the **slope** condition:\n\n- The slope of the **line segment** is $ m_{\\text{segment}} = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} $\n- The slope of the **tangent line** at the **midpoint** is $ m_{\\text{tangent}} = \\frac{2}{y_M} = \\frac{4}{y_1 + y_2} $\n\nWe are told that the **line segment is perpendicular to the tangent line**, so:\n\n$$\nm_{\\text{segment}} \\cdot m_{\\text{tangent}} = -1\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} \\cdot \\frac{4}{y_1 + y_2} = -1\n$$\n\nNow, as before:\n\n$$\nx_2 - x_1 = \\frac{y_2^2 - y_1^2}{4} = \\frac{(y_2 - y_1)(y_2 + y_1)}{4}\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y_2 - y_1}{\\frac{(y_2 - y_1)(y_2 + y_1)}{4}} \\cdot \\frac{4}{y_1 + y_2} = -1\n$$\n\nSimplify the first term:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y_2 - y_1}{\\frac{(y_2 - y_1)(y_2 + y_1)}{4}} = \\frac{4}{y_2 + y_1}\n$$\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{y_1 + y_2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{y_1 + y_2} = -1\n\\Rightarrow \\left( \\frac{4}{y_1 + y_2} \\right)^2 = -1\n$$\n\nThis is again **impossible**, as the square of a real number cannot be negative.\n\nSo this suggests that **there are no such circles**, but the problem says \"find the locus of the centers\".\n\nTherefore, we must have made a **mistake in the interpretation**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Try a different approach\n\nLet’s suppose the circle is centered at $ (a, b) $, and intersects the parabola $ y^2 = 4x $ at two points $ (x_1, y_1) $ and $ (x_2, y_2) $.\n\nWe are told that the **line segment** connecting these points is **perpendicular** to the **tangent line** at the **midpoint** of the chord.\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n- The **midpoint** of the chord is $ M = \\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right) $